Some of your patches are among the best I've seen.  Have you considered
submitting them formally to the DevTeam?  I seem to remember seeing a
request from them on the site, or maybe in the docs, asking for
patches.  Hm, don't take my word on this though, I'd hate to be the
result of them getting spammed with a hundred patches....

One of the cooler things about Slash'em that should make the transition
to the next version are the many little optional "lair" dungeon
branches.  There are a few items, the less bizarre ones, in that game
that could stand to be introduced into vanilla.

Things I would like to see:
- More experience levels.  Let's let the player go up to 50.
- Hand-in-hand with that, we need more high-level monsters.  Hell, we
need them even without a raised level cap; the only real
randomly-generated competition for high-level characters are Archons
and the tougher liches (and all liches can be taken out with a single
blessed genocide).
- A bigger dungeon, but only if it is more interesting (that is, not if
the new levels are going to be mazes).
- I'd also like to see the higher experience levels be earnable in ways
other than arbitrary level gain.  This is one of the most interesting
things about Slash'em.
- More room types, more level generation types, more branches, more
items, more more more.

Things from Slash'em that should NOT, IMO, be picked up by the DevTeam:
- Some of the weirder class-specific abilities.  I am thinking in
particular of the Monk ability in which the player has to enter
direction codes to activate some special attack.  I know where that
comes from, and I am not amused by video game references in Nethack.
The same goes for an Undead Slayer's proficiency in whips.
- Gypsys.
- Shopkeeper services.  Because to keep them balanced shopkeepers have
to be made vastly more powerful in order to prevent the player from
trivially using them then killing them to get their money back.
Shopkeepers could stand to be given a few more hit dice, but not *that*
many more.

- John H.


